# 0005. The password goes only to a hidden PAM prompt; logs and state stay private

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

The greeter runs before anyone has logged in, so it handles the password narrowly and keeps everything
it writes readable only by its owner. Security is the point of this repo.

## Technical details
- **Hidden prompt only**: the typed password answers a PAM prompt only when it is hidden (no echo);
  a visible prompt (a username, a code asked first) gets `unsupported prompt` instead of the
  password. The field is cleared after use and never logged.
- **No debug log of the reply**: Quickshell's greetd module logs every request at debug level,
  including the password reply, so the greeter starts with
  `qs --log-rules 'quickshell.service.greetd.debug=false'` and `umask 077`; its log is
  `/var/cache/archminimal-greeter/greeter.log`.
- **State**: `/var/cache/archminimal-greeter` is 0700, owned by `greeter` (`system/tmpfiles.conf`);
  last user and session in `state.json`. The greeter user has no writable home, so `XDG_*` dirs point
  there.
- **Session log**: each session's output goes to `~/.local/state/archminimal-session.log`, mode 600,
  overwritten at each login.
- **Session command**: greetd runs ONE shell string; `Sessions.qml` builds it with `shq()`
  (single-quote escaping), passing the `.desktop` `Exec` and session id as arguments, never spliced in
  unquoted.
- **No ways to start programs**: the greeter's Hyprland has no binds; anything added there runs as
  `greeter` before login.
- **Consequences**: no second-factor PAM prompts; a change to any of this needs a real login on the VM.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: greetd IPC, PAM, Quickshell Greetd service, systemd-tmpfiles.
